# Firmware build dependencies (CI jobs: "Device build", and the Release job that
# handles OTA_SIGNING_KEY). Compile with:
#   pip-compile --generate-hashes --output-file=requirements/build.txt requirements/build.in
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KNOWN, ACCEPTED: 5 Dependabot alerts on `starlette` (2 high, 2 medium, 1 low)
# Investigated 2026-07-26. Do not re-derive this; re-check only when the
# constraint below changes.
#
# THERE IS NO UPGRADE PATH. platformio 6.1.19 — the LATEST release — declares
# `starlette >=0.19, <0.53` (see its dependencies.py). Every patched starlette
# is >=1.0.1, i.e. outside that cap. Forcing one here breaks resolution; it does
# not fix anything.
#
# THE VULNERABLE CODE IS NEVER REACHED IN CI. All five advisories require a
# RUNNING Starlette HTTP server handling requests (StaticFiles SSRF, Host-header
# validation, form-size limits, HTTPEndpoint method dispatch). Only three
# platformio modules touch starlette:
#   * home/run.py, home/rpc/server.py — the `pio home` web UI. CI runs `pio run`
#     and never starts it.
#   * compat.py:42 — `if sys.version_info >= (3, 9): from asyncio import
#     to_thread`, ELSE starlette. CI pins Python 3.12, so it takes the asyncio
#     branch and starlette is not imported at all.
# Verified empirically: zero starlette/uvicorn modules load during `pio run`.
#
# WHY THE ALERTS APPEARED NOW: they are not new. platformio has always installed
# a vulnerable starlette in CI; before requirements/*.txt existed there was no
# manifest for Dependabot to read. Pinning made existing risk visible, which is
# the point of pinning.
#
# WHEN TO REVISIT: if platformio relaxes `<0.53`, drop this note and let
# Dependabot bump starlette normally.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
platformio
